blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:134:	The two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\code^{\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, coincides with the game $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. Theorem~4.7 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\mathsf{ldparams}$), therefore yields the present statement for $k = 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; in this development it is carried by Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal}, and Remark~\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} records the interface.
blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:137:\begin{remark}[Import status of Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}]\label{rem:ld-soundness-provider}
blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:138:	Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is an imported result: the paper derives it by reduction to the two-prover tensor-code test of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum} (Theorem~4.7 there, applied with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$), followed by the standard extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples. The intended provider here is Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal}, the quantum soundness of the low individual degree test (Definition~\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} of Chapter~\ref{chap:test}), which formalizes the result of~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}. The exact derivation of the present statement from the consistency conclusions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal} --- including the correspondence between the parameter tuple $\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ with $k=1$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\ref{chap:test}, the treatment of the typed question distribution, and the extension to general $k$ --- remains to be carried out and is recorded as a separate work item.
